---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import EAIUtilityBar from '../components/EAIUtilityBar.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PrepublicationFooter from '../components/PrepublicationFooter.astro';
import ReferenceDesk from '../components/ReferenceDesk.astro';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead
      title="America's School Trust Library — An evidentiary archive of America's school trust lands and funds"
      description="Lands and funds set aside since 1785 to support America's public K-12 schools."
    />
  </head>
  <body class="min-h-screen bg-archive-paper text-ink mode-library">
    <EAIUtilityBar />
    <Header />
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-5xl p-4 sm:p-8">
      <section class="home-declaration" aria-labelledby="home-declaration-heading">
        <h1 id="home-declaration-heading" class="home-declaration__title">
          America's School Trust Library
        </h1>
        <p class="home-declaration__subtitle">
          An evidentiary archive of America's school trust lands and funds
        </p>
        <p class="home-declaration__lede">
          If you are a parent, a school board member, or a neighbor of land you
          have driven past your whole life, this record is about you. Roughly
          twenty states still hold school trust land, and it is earning money
          right now. In many of them you cannot easily
          find out how much it earned last year, who decided what to do with
          it, or how much reached a classroom &mdash; not because the answer is
          secret, but because the record is scattered across state agencies,
          court files, and old statutes, and nobody has gathered it in one
          place.
        </p>

        <p class="home-declaration__lede">
          This Library gathers it and shows its sources, so that a parent at a
          school board meeting, a reporter on deadline, and a legislator
          drafting a bill can all check the same shelf and none of them has to
          take our word for it. Where a state has not
          published its accounting, the
          <a href="/counting/">Counting House</a> says so and names it: the
          silence is part of the record too.
        </p>

        <p class="home-declaration__paragraph">
          In 1785, two years before the Constitution and four years before
          George Washington took office, the Continental Congress passed a Land
          Ordinance that did something quietly radical: it set aside one parcel
          of land in every six-square-mile township of the new western
          territories, forever, to fund public schools. The schools didn't
          exist yet. The townships didn't exist yet. The states that would
          claim those townships didn't exist yet. But the principle did: before
          the United States organized itself into private property, it carved
          out a piece of the public domain for the public's children.
        </p>
        <p class="home-declaration__paragraph">
          By 1959, when Hawaii became the fiftieth state, the federal
          government had granted approximately 77 million acres for school
          trust purposes &mdash; an area larger than Italy. The state is the
          trustee. The schoolchildren of that state, present and future, are
          the beneficiaries.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="home-doors mt-10" data-reveal aria-label="Four doors into the Library">
        <ul class="home-doors__grid">
          <li class="home-door home-door--1">
            <a href="/states/" class="home-door__link">
              <span class="home-door__icon" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <path d="M20 10c0 6-8 12-8 12s-8-6-8-12a8 8 0 0 1 16 0"/>
                  <circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <h2 class="home-door__heading">Find your state</h2>
              <p class="home-door__subtitle">
                A dossier for every state in the union &mdash; start with your own.
              </p>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="home-door home-door--2">
            <a href="/counting/" class="home-door__link">
              <span class="home-door__icon" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <rect x="2" y="6" width="20" height="12" rx="2"/>
                  <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2.5"/>
                  <path d="M6 12h.01M18 12h.01"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <h2 class="home-door__heading">See the money</h2>
              <p class="home-door__subtitle">
                The Counting House compares the money, state by state.
              </p>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="home-door home-door--3">
            <a href="/court/" class="home-door__link">
              <span class="home-door__icon" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <path d="M12 7v14"/>
                  <path d="M3 18a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h5a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 4 4 0 0 1 4-4h5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-6a3 3 0 0 0-3 3 3 3 0 0 0-3-3z"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <h2 class="home-door__heading">Read the cases</h2>
              <p class="home-door__subtitle">
                The Court Room: rulings, filings, and case files.
              </p>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="home-door">
            <a href="/catalog/" class="home-door__link">
              <span class="home-door__icon" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"/>
                  <path d="m21 21-4.3-4.3"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <h2 class="home-door__heading">Browse the catalog</h2>
              <p class="home-door__subtitle">
                The public catalog: legal authorities, case-law texts,
                key figures, books, and special collections.
              </p>
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12" aria-label="Featured entry points" data-reveal>
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">
          Featured entries
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <li class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
              <a href="/reading/us-or/" class="text-blue-700 underline hover:text-blue-900">
                Oregon — the test case
              </a>
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
              Strong constitutional architecture (1857), the Elliott State Forest decoupling
              (2017–2022), and two separate proceedings: in the 23CV39056 line, the
              January 28, 2026 Court of Appeals standing decision, made final when the
              Oregon Supreme Court denied review in S072734 on July 23, 2026; and in
              Coos County Circuit Court 24CV38372, the July 24, 2026 summary judgment
              for the State.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
              <a href="/reading/us-ut/" class="text-blue-700 underline hover:text-blue-900">
                Utah — the trust under continuous defense
              </a>
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
              A national model for what an active constituency does: revenue routed
              school by school made the trust visible to parents, teachers, and
              principals, and a watchful crew now defends the architecture against
              successive diversion attempts.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
              <a href="/reading/us-ms/" class="text-blue-700 underline hover:text-blue-900">
                Mississippi — the lost inheritance
              </a>
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
              Section 16 history shows what depletion looks like when no recovery occurred.
              Sixteenth sections sold for as little as $1 per acre in the 1820s.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10 rounded-md border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 text-sm text-slate-700 shadow-sm" data-reveal aria-label="About this library">
        <p>
          America's School Trust Library is an Oregon nonprofit public benefit
          corporation. <a href="/librarians/" class="text-blue-700 underline">How
          this library is made &rarr;</a> &middot;
          <a href="/about/corrections/" class="text-blue-700 underline">Corrections
          ledger &rarr;</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <ReferenceDesk />

      {/* v9 D.2 — lobby floor inlay. Library Mode only; Reference and
          Standard Mode hide via mode-reference.css / default base.
          The seal image lands separately (Cowork-side); onerror hides
          gracefully until then. */}
      <div class="library-seal-inlay">
        <button
          type="button"
          class="library-seal-button"
          data-seal-trigger
          aria-label="View library seal larger"
        >
          <img
            src="/seal/library-seal-v1.png"
            alt="America's School Trust Library seal"
            class="library-seal library-seal--inlay"
            onerror="this.style.display='none'"
          />
        </button>
      </div>
    </main>
    <PrepublicationFooter />
    <Footer />
    <script>
      // 2026-07-02 re-skin: restrained scroll-reveal (parity with the
      // schooltrustlands.net redesign). Gates: prefers-reduced-motion is
      // honored, and without JS the html never gets .reveal-ready, so all
      // content stays fully visible.
      if (
        !window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches &&
        'IntersectionObserver' in window
      ) {
        document.documentElement.classList.add('reveal-ready');
        const io = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries) => {
            for (const entry of entries) {
              if (entry.isIntersecting) {
                entry.target.classList.add('is-in');
                io.unobserve(entry.target);
              }
            }
          },
          { rootMargin: '0px 0px -8% 0px' },
        );
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-reveal]').forEach((el) => io.observe(el));
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
